## DESCRIPTION
## Linear functions
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## BookTitle('Algebra: Form and Function')
## BookChapter('Linear functions, expressions, and equations')
## DBsubject(Algebra)
## DBchapter(Linear equations and functions)
## DBsection(Graphs of lines)
## Date(01/01/10)
## Institution(Fort Lewis College)
## Author(Paul Pearson)
## MLT(matchgraphfnc)
## Level(3)
## TitleText1('Algebra: Form and Function')
## AuthorText1('McCallum, Connally, and Hughes-Hallett')
## EditionText1('1')
## Section1('5.1')
## Problem1('33')
## KEYWORDS('linear functions')

#  Initialization

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'plots.pl', 'parserPopUp.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

$plot = Plot(
    xmin        => -1,
    ymin        => -1,
    ymax        =>  3,
    xtick_delta => 10,
    ytick_delta => 10,
    show_grid   => 0
);
$plot->add_function(
    [ '2-x',   'x', -5, 5, color => 'black' ],
    [ '2-x/2', 'x', -5, 5, color => 'red' ],
    [ '1',     'x', -5, 5, color => 'orange' ],
    [ 'x/2',   'x', -5, 5, color => 'blue' ],
    [ 'x/2-1', 'x', -5, 5, color => 'green' ]
);
$plot->add_label(
    [ -0.5, 2.75, 'A', color => 'black' ],
    [ 0.75, 1.75, 'B', color => 'red' ],
    [ -0.5, 1.15, 'C', color => 'orange' ],
    [ 3,    1.75, 'D', color => 'blue' ],
    [ 0.75, -0.4, 'E', color => 'green' ],
);

# s = 1
# k = 0.5

@eqn       = ('2s - x', '2s - k x', 's', 'k x', 'k x - s');
@perm      = random_subset(5, 0 .. 4);
@dropdowns = map { DropDown([ 'A' .. 'E' ], $perm[$_]) } (0 .. 4);

BEGIN_PGML
Match each function with its graph A-E.  The constants [`k`] and [`s`] are the same in each function.

[#
    [.
    [_]{$dropdowns[0]} [` f(x) = [$eqn[$perm[0]]] `]

    [_]{$dropdowns[1]} [` f(x) = [$eqn[$perm[1]]] `]

    [_]{$dropdowns[2]} [` f(x) = [$eqn[$perm[2]]] `]

    [_]{$dropdowns[3]} [` f(x) = [$eqn[$perm[3]]] `]

    [_]{$dropdowns[4]} [` f(x) = [$eqn[$perm[4]]] `]
    .] [.[!!]{$plot}{400} .]
#]{align => 'lc'}

END_PGML

@letters = ('A' .. 'E')[@perm];

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
First, note that function C is a constant function, so the value of [`s`] is a positive number.  This must be [`[$eqn[2]]`].  

Also, functions  D and E are parallel, with D passing through the origin.  These are [`[$eqn[3]]`] and [`[$eqn[4]]`] respectively.

The last two functions are A and B and the equations are [`[$eqn[0]]`] and [`[$eqn[1]]`].  The only difference is the slopes.   This can be determined by noting that functions B, C and D all intersect at the same point. This must mean that B has slope [`-k`], so this is [`[$eqn[1]]`] and therefore A is [`[$eqn[0]]`].
END_PGML_SOLUTION
ENDDOCUMENT();
